INT. FISH AUCTION HALL - 4 A.M.

A cavernous concrete shed, wet floor, floodlights humming over rows of ice-packed crates. The air smells of brine and diesel. A digital auction board above the pit blinks red numbers that nobody is watching.

ANSELM KRAUSE (50s, heavy-set, oilskin coat, a gold tooth catching the light) sits on an overturned crate at the center. He turns a fish scale in his fingers.

Across from him stands CORRIGAN BLACK (40s, narrow, long camel coat out of place among rubber boots). He holds a leather briefcase against his chest.

Behind Corrigan, DESSA OYELARAN (30s, sharp bob, auctioneer's clipboard) watches the doors. Next to her, BAO TRAN (20s, forklift driver, earbuds dangling) fidgets with a pallet jack.

EVERETT SLOANE (60s, ex-harbor police, grey stubble, cheap suit) leans against a pillar with a cigarette he has not lit. FITZ MORROW (20s, tackle-shop clerk, sweating through a windbreaker) crouches by the loading door.

ANSELM
You are four minutes late.

CORRIGAN
The tide table lied.

ANSELM
Tide tables don't lie. Men do.

Corrigan sets the briefcase on the crate. Doesn't open it.

CORRIGAN
It's all there.

ANSELM
I'll count it.

EVERETT
(from the pillar)
Count it faster.

Dessa shifts her weight. Her eyes stay on the doors.

DESSA
Truck's idling on the east dock. If it moves, I move.

BAO
(pulling out an earbud)
Is this still the tuna thing?

DESSA
Put your earbud back in, Bao.

Bao obeys, too quickly.

Anselm snaps the clasps. Counts. His lips move. Corrigan watches Anselm's hands, not the money.

CORRIGAN
It's the full amount.

ANSELM
It's a full amount.

Fitz stands. The knees of his jeans are wet.

FITZ
We should go. Both of you. Now.

EVERETT
Sit down, Fitz.

FITZ
Everett, the boats are coming back in. I can hear the winches.

Everett finally lights the cigarette.

EVERETT
Then we have until the first crate hits the ice.

Anselm closes the briefcase. Slides it under the crate with his boot.

ANSELM
(to Corrigan)
He doesn't trust you.

CORRIGAN
Which "he"?

ANSELM
Pick one.

Corrigan glances at Everett. At Fitz. Nobody blinks.

Somewhere in the dark, a winch screams. A crate crashes onto the concrete. Water sloshes across the floor toward the five pairs of boots and one pair of leather shoes.

Nobody moves.
